====================================================================================================
 (2) CUT + CYCLE = ALEXANDER/HODGE DUALITY of the arc space F_2^{C(n,2)}
====================================================================================================
   n C(n,2) arcs cut dim (n-1) cycle dim C(n-1,2)  sum=arcs? cut⊥cycle?
   4           6             3                  3       True       True
   5          10             4                  6       True       True
   6          15             5                 10       True       True
   7          21             6                 15       True       True
   8          28             7                 21       True       True
  => arc space = cut (n-1) (+) cycle (C(n-1,2)), orthogonal complements: the GF(2) Alexander/Hodge split
     (cut=coboundary=tournament/chromatic; cycle=even-graph=flow). THM-584 R-even/R-odd = this duality.

====================================================================================================
 (3) THE EVEN-GRAPH CODE = cycle space of K_n = an LDPC code
====================================================================================================
   n length C(n,2) dim C(n-1,2) #checks (vtx) check wt (n-1)  min dist
   4             6            3             4              3         3
   5            10            6             5              4         3
   6            15           10             6              5         3
   7            21           15             7              6         3
   8            28           21             8              7         3
  => n LOCAL parity checks (even-degree per vertex), each of weight n-1; distance 3 (triangle). An LDPC.
     Dinur et al. build codes where LOCAL tests certify GLOBAL membership with O(1) queries. The tournament
     'code' (iso class from local invariants) does the OPPOSITE: local certification FAILS at n=7 (S14-19
     reconstruction wall) => the metagraph is an ANTI-LTC -- query complexity GROWS with n.

====================================================================================================
 (1) THE SQUARE/CUBE COMPLEX + half-tiling fold
====================================================================================================
  n=5: m=6 tiles on a staircase grid (3 rows x up to 3 cols); row-flips A + column-flips B => square complex over F_2^6 (ABELIAN). half-tiling (σ-fixed) = sub-cube dim 4.
  n=6: m=10 tiles on a staircase grid (4 rows x up to 4 cols); row-flips A + column-flips B => square complex over F_2^10 (ABELIAN). half-tiling (σ-fixed) = sub-cube dim 6.
  n=7: m=15 tiles on a staircase grid (5 rows x up to 5 cols); row-flips A + column-flips B => square complex over F_2^15 (ABELIAN). half-tiling (σ-fixed) = sub-cube dim 9.
  => the tiling cube is a genuine SQUARE (cube) complex with two flip-directions = an ABELIAN left-right
     Cayley complex; it is a product/torus, NOT the nonabelian EXPANDER LTC. Nonabelian upgrade = the S_n
     quotient (Schreier) or a PSL_2 realization -- that is where expansion/testability would live.

VERDICT: the half-tiling IS a left-right SQUARE complex (row x column flips over the cycle space), and the
 cut-cycle Alexander duality is the Tutte chromatic<->flow / THM-584 R-even<->R-odd split. But it is the
 ABELIAN version: the even-graph LDPC code is locally CHECKABLE, and the tournament reconstruction is an
 ANTI-LTC (local testing fails at n=7). The LTC lead points to: seek a NONABELIAN (S_n / PSL_2) realization
 where the tiling square complex becomes an EXPANDER and reconstruction becomes O(1)-locally-testable.
DONE.
